                    persist_oneoff:         `A campaign push — hit 50% in the survey window the board reads; month six comes later`,
                    r4_recognition_label:   `How do you reinforce the new behavior?`,
                    recog_recognition:      `Ongoing public recognition of teams, tied to who Hanseatic is, with progress fed back`,
                    recog_prizes:           `Prizes for the leading teams and a leaderboard through the campaign — competition gets people moving`,

                    warn_intensity_range:   `Campaign intensity must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_posture_range:     `The posture weight must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_alloc_negative:    `Budget allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_alloc_total:       `The engagement budget must total exactly 600.000 € (600 in thousands). Re-balance the levers.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_emotion:       `<strong>That’s the right diagnosis.</strong> 71% already care — this is a value-action gap, not an information deficit, and the emotion blocking action differs by segment. Now your frame can target the actual emotion instead of preaching to the converted.`,
                    coach_r1_info:          `<strong>You’ve planned a beautiful emissions infographic.</strong> But your own survey says 71% already care — they’re not short of information, they’re short of action. Adding facts to people who already agree changes nothing. What emotion is sitting between caring and doing, and which group is it stuck in?`,
                    coach_r1_opposition:    `<strong>Calling it pure opposition is a trap.</strong> Most of these people support the goal — pushing harder on supporters reads as coercion and breeds the very resistance you fear. Separate the anxious (who can be brought along) from the genuinely opposed before you decide who to push.`,
                    coach_r1_routearound:   `<strong>Routing around the works council treats unmanaged fear as hard opposition.</strong> Two of three reps speak for anxious members — address the fear and they come with you; go around them and you manufacture the resistance you were trying to avoid.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as an emotional value-action gap AND mapped the right emotion to each segment — fear in production, guilt-fatigue in the office, manageable fear (not opposition) in the works council. That map is what lets you match the frame to the emotion in Round 2.`,
                    coach_r2_hope:          `<strong>The hope/efficacy frame is the durable choice.</strong> “Here’s how our actions add up and what each of us can change” builds the self- and collective-efficacy that fear and guilt erode. It lands slower than an alarm, so pair it with the visible quick-wins in Round 4 — but it’s the frame that survives past the campaign.`,
                    coach_r2_pride:         `<strong>Pride/identity plays to your strongest asset.</strong> “Clean origin is who Hanseatic is” turns the change into living the company’s values, not a threat imposed on it. Identity framing tolerates more volume than fear and reaches the guilt-fatigued who tuned the doom messaging out.`,
                    coach_r2_fear:          `<strong>You led with “the planet is in crisis, act now.”</strong> Attention spiked — and your production segment, already 48% anxious about layoffs, just heard “crisis = cuts.” Avoidance went up, not down. Fear buys a headline and costs you the people you most need to change. What would hope/efficacy do to that same segment?`,
                    coach_r2_guilt:         `<strong>More guilt doesn’t reach the guilt-fatigued.</strong> Telling employees their footprint harms their children’s future confirms exactly why the 34% who’ve tuned out stopped listening. Guilt has diminishing returns here — what emotion re-engages an audience that’s already heard the sermon?`,
                    coach_r2_uniformthreat: `<strong>One threat frame for everyone hits the anxious segment hardest.</strong> A uniform fear/guilt campaign lands on the people least able to absorb it. At minimum, tailor a hope + job-security frame to production before the threat framing turns their anxiety into works-council resistance.`,
                    coach_r2_norisk:        `<strong>“A strong frame just works” is how leaders walk into the backfire.</strong> Every frame has an emotional risk — fear deepens avoidance, guilt fatigues, hope lands slowly. Naming the risk is what lets you manage it. Which segment is your frame most likely to lose?`,
                    coach_r3_validate:      `<strong>Validating the fear first is the right move.</strong> Naming it and taking it seriously before you reframe is what turns anxiety into agency. The line worker felt heard, so the reframe — threat into control — actually landed instead of bouncing off.`,
                    coach_r3_spreadsheet:   `<strong>You answered a feeling with a spreadsheet.</strong> A line worker said “you’re replacing us with machines” and you replied with the decarbonization roadmap. The fear didn’t go away — it went quiet and turned into resistance with the works council. Acknowledging the fear first would have moved your regulation score, not the data.`,
                    coach_r3_dismiss:       `<strong>Dismissing the rumor doesn’t kill it — it buries it.</strong> Telling anxious staff “that’s not what this is about” and moving on tells them their fear isn’t welcome. It goes underground and re-emerges as hardened works-council resistance. The feeling needed to be met, not waved away.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>“No one will lose their job” calmed the room — and you can’t keep it.</strong> The board hasn’t guaranteed it and the works council will hold you to it. When the first role changes, your credibility collapses and every future message is discounted. Give people control and honesty without a promise you can’t keep.`,
                    coach_r3_deflect:       `<strong>Deflecting to the board reads as “I won’t protect you.”</strong> Anxious staff asked you a direct question and you stepped aside. You don’t need to make promises you can’t keep — but you do need to own the conversation, commit to consultation, and give people a real sense of control.`,
                    coach_r3_moreguilt:     `<strong>“The future depends on this” is more sermon to the sermon-weary.</strong> Reminding the guilt-fatigued of the stakes is the messaging they already tuned out. What re-engages them is efficacy — visible proof that their actions add up — not another round of stakes.`,
                    coach_r3_rational:      `<strong>Staying strictly rational in a charged room leaves the emotion unmanaged.</strong> The feelings don’t disappear because you didn’t address them — they harden. Validating emotion before redirecting isn’t soft; it’s what makes the redirect work.`,
                    coach_r3_playbook:      `<strong>That’s an emotion-regulation playbook.</strong> Validate the fear before reframing, give credible reassurance with real control instead of a hollow guarantee, and restore efficacy to the guilt-fatigued. Anxiety became engagement, and the works council came with you instead of against you.`,
                    coach_r4_durable:       `<strong>This is built to last.</strong> Peer champions and a live dashboard create social proof and a feedback loop; ongoing recognition tied to identity makes the new behavior a norm rather than a stunt. When the budget and the campaign end, the behavior holds — that’s durable change, not a spike.`,
                    coach_r4_oneoff:        `<strong>Your challenge week hit a number — and then it’s week six.</strong> The leaderboard is gone, the prizes stopped, and behavior is sliding back. You bought a spike, not a habit. What in the design — social proof, a visible dashboard, peer champions — makes the new behavior stick after the enthusiasm fades?`,
                    coach_r4_prizes:        `<strong>One-time prizes reinforce the prize, not the behavior.</strong> When the leaderboard ends, so does the reason to act. Recognition tied to identity and continuous feedback keeps the behavior meaningful after the campaign budget is spent.`,
                    coach_r4_nofeedback:    `<strong>You funded the noise but not the feedback loop.</strong> With almost nothing on peer champions or a progress dashboard, there’s no social proof and no visible efficacy — so even a big uptake has nothing to hold it in place. Fund the mechanisms that make change persist.`,
                    coach_r4_mixed:         `<strong>A reasonable spread</strong> — but tilt it toward what persists. The more weight on peer champions, the efficacy dashboard and credible recognition, and the less on a one-off gamified spike, the more of this behavior survives past the campaign.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed the value-action gap; mapped emotion to segment.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Read it as facts or opposition, not emotion.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Hope/identity frame with the emotional risk named.`,
                    sum_r2_threat:          `Defaulted to a fear/guilt frame on anxious staff.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Frame chosen without managing its emotional risk.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Validated fear, reassured credibly, restored efficacy.`,
                    sum_r3_hollow:          `Calmed the room with a promise you can’t keep.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Answered feelings with facts or deflection.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Funded persistence — champions, dashboard, recognition.`,
                    sum_r4_oneoff:          `Bought a one-off spike with no feedback loop.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Engagement design too thin on persistence.`,

                    insight_durable:        `Durable engagement: you treated it as an emotional problem, matched the frame to each segment, validated fear before reframing it, and funded the mechanisms — peer champions, an efficacy dashboard, recognition — that make new behavior a norm. The behavior change holds after the budget and the campaign end.`,
                    insight_backfire:       `Threat frame backfired: a fear/guilt-heavy campaign on an already-anxious, guilt-fatigued workforce spiked attention and deepened avoidance. Resistance hardened in the works council, and the people you most needed to change are the ones who tuned out.`,
                    insight_spike:          `A spike, not a habit: you hit the participation number during the campaign, but with little social proof, no live feedback loop and one-off prizes, the behavior is already sliding back. The board target was met on paper and lost in practice.`,
                    insight_short:          `The value-action gap moved only partway. Some of the emotional groundwork or the persistence design was missing, so caring still hasn’t fully translated into sustained action.`,
                    insight_belowtarget:    `You finished below the board’s 50% target. The gap between 22% and 50% is closed by emotion, efficacy and social proof — not by more facts or a louder alarm.`,
                    insight_attarget:       `You reached the board’s 50% participation target — at least one concrete sustainability action adopted by half the workforce.`,
                    insight_overpromise:    `You gave a flat job guarantee to calm the room. It worked in Round 3: regulation rose. It is not yours to give: the board has not made it and the works council held you to it, so in Round 4 it cost 8 points of regulation, 10 of message effectiveness and 14 of resistance, and the index carries a credibility charge.`,
                    insight_fearframe:      `You led with a threat frame (fear or guilt). Where 48% of production staff already report anxiety about job losses, a threat frame raises the threat without a route to act on it. Volume is part of the frame, not a separate dial: the louder it ran, the more message effectiveness it lost and the more resistance it added.`,
                    insight_trusted:        `Trust is high and resistance low — anxiety became agency rather than resistance. That is emotion-aware leadership working: the works council came with you, and the behavior change rests on belief, not pressure.`,

                    hint_fear:              `Your frame leaned on fear or guilt and resistance is climbing — exactly the backfire the anxious and guilt-fatigued segments produce. Shift toward hope, efficacy and identity, and validate the fear directly.`,
                    hint_overpromise:       `You promised job security you can’t guarantee. Pull back to credible reassurance — honesty about what you can promise, plus consultation, reskilling and real choice. Hollow promises collapse on the first role change.`,
                    hint_oneoff:            `Your design is a one-off spike with no feedback loop. Re-weight toward peer champions, a live progress dashboard and ongoing recognition so the behavior survives after the prizes stop.`,
                    hint_resistance:        `Resistance is dangerously high — the cynicism spiral and works-council pushback are compounding. Validate emotion before reframing and stop relying on threat framing.`,
                    hint_behavior:          `Behavior change is well short of 50% with little time left. Match the frame to each segment’s emotion and fund the efficacy mechanisms — champions, dashboard, quick-wins — that actually move action.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — behavior change near target, low resistance and high regulation. Lock it in with persistence mechanisms so the new behavior becomes the norm.`,

